__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.venv/
venv/
env/
.DS_Store
*.log

# Vercel: the linked project, and anything holding credentials.
.vercel/
.env*
# ...except the template. Three documents (docs/SETUP.md, docs/DEPLOYMENT.md,
# docs/PLAN.md) tell the reader to copy it, so a deployment that does not
# carry it cannot be configured from the repository alone. It holds only
# placeholders — "replace-me-with-32-bytes-of-hex" and localhost URLs.
!.env.example

# Written by the CLI during a local `vercel build`; the remote build writes its own.
# Nothing here is part of the project — the functions are stdlib-only, which
# requirements.txt says in as many words.
.python-version

# Studio runtime state. var/studio holds the SQLite database, uploaded objects,
# and — when STUDIO_ENCRYPTION_KEY is unset — a generated key file. None of that
# belongs in version control.
var/
web/node_modules/
# web/dist is a build output, and build outputs do not normally belong in a
# repository. It is committed deliberately here: the Vercel deployment serves the
# client from the Python function and has no Node build step, so without it a
# deployed Studio has an API and no page. Rebuild with scripts/build_client.py,
# which fails if the mount prefix is missing or a called path is not routed.
!web/dist/

# Local working copies and session artifacts. The committed `gen_studio_env.py`
# / `run_studio.py` / `smoke_studio.py` / `repro_assets_list.py` are the real
# entry points; the files below are what those scripts produce or what
# interactive sessions leave behind.
.studio_env.sh
prompt-studio.env
out.txt
smoke_out.txt
start_studio.bat
git_status.txt
git_log.txt
git_branch.txt
git_remote.txt
git_porcelain.txt
gitignore_diff.txt
audit_studio.txt
audit_tests.txt
audit_web.txt
*.out.txt
vbuild.txt
build_log.txt
result.txt
final.txt
.vercel
